"Fair distribution of bundles (https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.13421)\nciteturn9academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Title: Fair distribution of bundles\nAuthors: Pablo Soberón\nDate: Thu Jul 17 14:46:42 2025\n\nIn this paper, we study the problem of splitting fairly bundles of items.\nTitle: Fair distribution of bundles\nAuthors: Pablo Soberón\nDate: Thu Jul 17 14:46:42 2025\n\nIn this paper, we study the problem of splitting fairly bundles of items. We show that given $n$ bundles with $m$ kinds of items in them, it is possible to distribute the value of each kind of item fairly among $r$ persons by breaking apart at most $(r-1)m$ bundles. Moreover, we can guarantee that each participant will receive roughly $n/r - mr/2$ full bundles. The proof methods are topological and use a modified form of the configuration space/test map scheme. We obtain optimal results when $r$ is a power of two.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFair distribution of bundles - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2507.13421v1)\nciteturn9search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.3 years ago; Crawled: last month; ## Fair distribution of bundles\n\nPablo Soberón\n\nPublished: 2025-07-17 ... In this paper, we study the problem of splitting fairly bundles of items.\n\n## Fair distribution of bundles\n\nPablo Soberón\n\nPublished: 2025-07-17\n\nComments: 13 pages, 3 figures\n\nNote:\n\nPublication:\n\nconfiguration space\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nIn this paper, we study the problem of splitting fairly bundles of items. We show that given $n$ bundles with $m$ kinds of items in them, it is possible to distribute the value of each kind of item fairly among $r$ persons by breaking apart at most $(r-1)m$ bundles. Moreover, we can guarantee that each participant will receive roughly $n/r - mr/2$ full bundles. The proof methods are topological and use a modified form of the configuration space/test map scheme. We obtain optimal results when $r$ is a power of two.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAlgebraic connectivity in normed spaces - University of Galway (https://research.universityofgalway.ie/en/publications/algebraic-connectivity-in-normed-spaces-2/)\nciteturn9search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; The algebraic connectivity of a graph G in a finite dimensional real normed linear space X is a geometric counterpart to the Fiedler number of the graph and can be regarded as a measure of the rigidity of the graph in X. ... Cruickshank, J, Dewar, S & Kitson, D 2026, 'Algebraic connectivity in normed spaces', Linear Algebra and Its Applications, vol. 739, pp.\n\n# Algebraic connectivity in normed spaces\n\n  * James Cruickshank\n  * , Sean Dewar\n  * , Derek Kitson\n\n  * School of Mathematical and Statistical Sciences\n\n  * University of Bristol\n  * Mary Immaculate College\n\nResearch output: Contribution to a Journal (Peer & Non Peer) › Article › peer-review\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe algebraic connectivity of a graph G in a finite dimensional real normed linear space X is a geometric counterpart to the Fiedler number of the graph and can be regarded as a measure of the rigidity of the graph in X. We analyse the behaviour of the algebraic connectivity of G in X with respect to graph decomposition, vertex deletion and isometric isomorphism, and provide a general bound expressed in terms of the geometry of X and the Fiedler number of the graph. Particular focus is given to the space ℓ_{∞}^{d} where we present explicit formulae and calculations as well as upper and lower bounds. As a key tool, we show that the monochrome subgraphs of a complete framework in ℓ_{∞}^{d} are odd-hole-free. Connections to redundant rigidity are also presented.\n\nOriginal language  | English\nPages (from-to)  | 10-42\nNumber of pages  | 33\nJournal  | Linear Algebra and Its Applications\nVolume  | 739\n